The relay agent receives the client's broadcast and
forwards the BOOTP request toward the BOOTP server, allowing it to cross the router/network boundary.

So when exactly does the relay agent come into play?
Only when the BOOTP server is not on the client's local network.

The key problem is:

Client's BOOTP request is a broadcast → router won't normally forward it → 
relay agent receives the broadcast and forwards it to the remote BOOTP server.


